      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Makan hati\" translates roughly to \"to feel heartbroken\" or \"to take it to heart,\" so it carries a very strong emotional connotation. Using it without a deep cultural context risks sounding awkward or overly familiar. For safe rapport-building, consider a general phrase acknowledging the vibrant industry or the importance of their local market instead. This shows respect without veering into complex idioms.",
      "source": "target",
